                <pb facs="0517" n="423" sameAs="frus:frus1930v03/pg423"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.124a/96: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Liberia</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reber</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Monrovia</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-08-26T16:00:00-00:44">August 26, 1930—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 26—3:20 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>109. I saw President King today who promised to discuss the question of funds for
                    the continuance sanitary campaign with the Secretary of the Treasury and give me
                    his reply within two days. He has verbally given me his assurance that Dr. Smith
                    will be granted the authority necessary to carry on his work when money is
                    available. The President seemed anxious to gain time to formulate an answer,
                    pending a Cabinet meeting this afternoon.</p>
                <p>It is understood this meeting was called to discuss an answer to the last letter
                    from the Fiscal Agents.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Reber</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
